// An array, which is the one composite whose layout no `repr` may choose
// (Ch. 2 §3).
//
// `[T; N]` is N elements of `T`, each at `i × size_of::<T>()`, with the
// array's alignment its element's and its size N times it. There is no
// padding *between* elements and none at the end, because an element's size
// is already a multiple of its alignment — so the arithmetic indexing does
// is the definition and not a consequence of it. Which is why this is fixed
// even under `repr(lang)`, where a struct's fields may be put anywhere.
//
// Nothing here reads an element. Where an array *sits* is a fact about the
// type and needs no inference, which is what makes it the part both
// implementations can answer before either can lower `&[T]`.

struct P { a: t27, b: t9 }

// An element narrower than a word: alignment 1, and a size that is not a
// multiple of three.
struct Bytes { xs: [t9; 5] }

// A word each.
struct Words { xs: [t27; 3] }

// An element that is itself laid out, and whose size already carries its
// own padding — six, not four, so the array is twelve.
struct Rows { xs: [P; 2] }

// **No** elements, which is a size of nothing and an alignment all the
// same: an alignment is a fact about the element type, and there is one
// whether or not any element exists.
struct Empty { xs: [t27; 0] }

// An array beside a scalar, so that what the two implementations agree
// about is the *offsets* and not only the size — a field may be put
// anywhere under `repr(lang)`, and the answer here is that the array goes
// first.
struct Mixed { a: t9, xs: [t27; 2] }

// An array of arrays. The inner one is a type like any other, which is the
// whole of what this asks.
struct Grid { xs: [[t9; 2]; 3] }

// And the part that is not arithmetic: an array's spare values are its
// **first element's**, at that element's offset — so an array of something
// with a niche has one and an array of something without has none. Every
// one of these is an enum, because a tag is where a niche is visible
// (Ch. 2 §5).

// `bool` leaves 19 681 of a tryte's patterns unused, so `None` is one of
// them and no tag is stored.
enum MaybeFlags { Some([bool; 2]), None }

// One element is still an array, and still lends its niche.
enum MaybeOne { Some([bool; 1]), None }

// `trit` the same, through three elements.
enum MaybeTrits { Some([trit; 3]), None }

// `t9` uses every pattern of its tryte, so an array of them has nothing
// spare and the tag is stored.
enum MaybeBytes { Some([t9; 2]), None }

// No elements is no first element, so there is no niche to take even
// though `t27` would have had one.
enum MaybeNone { Some([t27; 0]), None }

// A reference is never null, which is a niche the array passes on.
struct Held { p: &t27 }

enum MaybeHeld { Some([Held; 2]), None }

fn main() -> t27 { 0 }
